{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California tenant screening fee cap","requirement":"A landlord may charge no more than the state's maximum tenant screening fee ($68.96 in 2026), must give the applicant a copy of the credit report within 7 days, a receipt and itemized costs, and refund any unused portion of the fee.","key_value":"$68.96 maximum for 2026","coverage_conditions":"All residential rental applicants; the page states no building age, size or owner limits.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026","valid_through":"2026","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"The maximum tenant screening fee for 2026 is $68.96","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California reusable screening report and applicant-order rules","requirement":"A landlord must use a reusable screening report a tenant provides, may not charge a screening fee when no rental unit is available, and must refund the fee to applicants not selected unless it reviews applications in the order received.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental applicants; the page states no building age, size or owner limits.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"If the tenant provides a reusable screening report, the landlord must use it. The landlord cannot charge a prospective tenant a screening fee if no rental unit is actually available.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Berkeley tenant screening fee disclosure and non-refundable fee ban","requirement":"An owner must give a tenant screening fee rights statement and the state fee cap in or before the application, and may not charge an existing tenant a non-refundable fee to renew a tenancy or to add or replace a roommate.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental agreements in Berkeley, regardless of contrary lease language; applies to owners and their agents.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Any provision of an existing rental agreement or lease that violates the chapter is unenforceable.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.78","quoted_span":"An owner of residential rental property (or the owner’s agent) cannot charge a non-refundable fee to any existing tenant for the purpose of renewing a tenancy, in whole or in part, including any fee associated with the departure of a roommate or to request to add or replace a roommate in a pre-existing household.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","n_rules":3,"note":null}

